### I. ARCHITECTURAL SUMMARY OF THE AMENDMENT

This constitutional amendment remedies a critical operational and nomothetic vulnerability in **Article 7.3 (Bit-for-Bit Deterministic Reproducibility and Diverse Toolchain Verification)**.

#### 1. The Vulnerability Vector (`SV-2_CRITICAL`)
Under original Article 7.3.3, any binary distribution whose cryptographic hash *"cannot be independently reproduced from public source trees by any citizen node is instantly de-authorized from public infrastructure."* This statutory phrasing introduced two fatal operational vulnerabilities:
1. **Unilateral Denial-of-Service (DoS) / Epistemological Impasse:** Interpreting the phrase as an individual veto allows a single misconfigured, faulty, or adversarial citizen node to unilaterally de-authorize critical infrastructure firmware. Conversely, interpreting the phrase as a universal negative creates an unprovable threshold where backdoored binaries cannot be revoked until every node on Earth fails verification.
2. **Supervisory Control Severance & Operational Vacuum:** Mandating that unverified distributions are *"instantly de-authorized"* without specifying an automated, bumpless safe-mode fallback to a known-good baseline creates sudden supervisory software severance. While Article 7.1.4 prevents hydraulic cavitation and valve hammering through hardwired analog rate-limiters, dropping supervisory control without an authenticated safe-state build baseline ($B_0$) risks grid telemetry collapse, uncoordinated breaker trips, and transient phase-angle instabilities.

#### 2. Nomothetic & Engineering Resolution
The amendment executes a surgical two-part statutory correction:
- **Byzantine Multi-Node Consensus:** Replaces the unilateral single-node trigger with an objective consensus requirement (*"verified across independent builder attestations"*), preventing single-node extortion or DoS attacks.
- **Automated Bumpless Fallback to Baseline $B_0$:** Explicitly mandates that upon verified hash divergence, running systems deterministically execute an automated, bumpless safe-mode reversion to the preceding cryptographically authenticated, immutable baseline ($B_0$). In industrial control systems, a "bumpless" reversion guarantees that internal supervisory states and control setpoints do not jump discontinuously, preserving continuous operational monitoring and Tier 1 survival flows.
- **Acronym Compliance & Concision Trimming:** Expands the unexpanded acronym "ISA" in Article 7.3.2 to **Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)**, while eliminating duplicative phrasing. The surgical drop-in yields a net reduction of 3 words, bringing the total word count of Chapter VII from 880 words to **877 words**—safely within the mandatory 600–900 word constitutional concision boundary.

