### II. LINE-BY-LINE STATUTORY JUSTIFICATION

- **Article 7.3.2:**
  - *Original text:* `...rather than cross-ISA binary hash identity across distinct processor instruction sets.`
  - *Amended text:* `...rather than cross-Instruction Set Architecture (ISA) hash identity.`
  - *Justification:* Fulfills the constitutional mandate for immediate technical acronym expansion on first appearance. Eliminates redundant trailing phrasing (*"across distinct processor instruction sets"*) and streamlines introductory nouns (*"Foundational kernels"*, *"independent open-source toolchains"*), reducing the clause from 55 words to 45 words (-10 words).

- **Article 7.3.3:**
  - *Original text:* `Public repositories shall maintain decentralized, cryptographically auditable build records. Any binary distribution whose compiled cryptographic hash cannot be independently reproduced from public source trees by any citizen node is instantly de-authorized from public infrastructure.`
  - *Amended text:* `Public repositories shall maintain decentralized build records. If binary non-reproducibility from public source trees is verified across independent builder attestations, the unverified distribution is barred; running systems deterministically execute an automated, bumpless safe-mode reversion to the preceding authenticated, immutable baseline ($B_0$).`
  - *Justification:* Neutralizes the single-node DoS vector by replacing *"by any citizen node"* with *"verified across independent builder attestations"*. Replaces uncoordinated instantaneous severance (*"instantly de-authorized"*) with a deterministic, bumpless reversion to the preceding authenticated immutable baseline ($B_0$). This guarantees uninterrupted supervisory state control without administrative delays or committee overhead. The clause expands from 34 words to 41 words (+7 words).

- **Net Word Budget Balance:**
  $-10\text{ words (Art. 7.3.2)} + 7\text{ words (Art. 7.3.3)} = -3\text{ words net}$. Total Chapter VII word count shifts from 880 words to **877 words**, preserving the 600–900 word constitutional invariant.

